<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas — Halachah 5<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Part 3: §§ 27–40</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Continuation of <span class="v">"L'Hamshich Shalom"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 14</span>)</span></div>

<!-- §27 -->
<div class="sn">§ 27 — The Name <i>Nachal</i>: Acronym of <i>Ner Chanukah</i></div>

<div class="p">The kavanos [mystical intentions] teach that the essential illumination of <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span> is through the Holy Name <span class="h">Nachal</span> — the acronym of <span class="h">L'hadlik Ner Chanukah</span> [נ.ח.ל]. This = the Torah, which is a <span class="h">nachal novei'a</span> [flowing stream]: <span class="v">"as streams flowing"</span>; <span class="v">"myriads of streams of oil"</span> (Michah 6). The essence of the kindling: drawing the holiness of the oil (= the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> of Torah) without ceasing — <span class="v">"a spring that ever strengthens, a river that does not cease"</span>; <span class="v">"oil upon your head never be lacking"</span> — never to be broken away from Torah, no matter what.</div>

<div class="p">This is the meaning of: <span class="v">"this is the work of the <span class="h">Menorah</span> — hammered gold, from its base to its flowers, hammered (<span class="h">mikshah</span>)"</span> — from the root meaning stubbornness and perseverance: one must be <span class="kc">an enormous stubborn one in serving Hashem</span> from beginning to end. And: <span class="v">"the jar of flour shall not be consumed, and the cruse of oil shall not be lacking"</span> (I Melachim 17) — <span class="mx">"the jar of flour shall not be consumed"</span> through <span class="mx">"the cruse of oil shall not be lacking"</span> = through constant Torah-engagement, the curse of Adam's sin is reversed and blessing flows.</div>

<!-- §28 -->
<div class="sn">§ 28 — The Vision of the <i>Menorah</i>: Awakening from Sleep</div>

<div class="p">In the <span class="h">Haftarah</span> of <span class="h">Chanukah</span> (Zechariah), when the prophet saw the vision of the holy <span class="h">Menorah</span>: <span class="v">"he awakened me like a man awakened from his sleep."</span> The <span class="h">Menorah</span> = bringing illumination from the root-souls in Torah = the aspect of awakening from the sleep mentioned in <span class="lr">LM I:14</span> — raising souls from the state of <span class="v">"I am asleep"</span> [blemished souls before they return]. Because the <span class="h">Menorah</span>-vision involved this awakening, the verse says specifically <span class="v">"he awakened me like a man awakened from his sleep."</span></div>

<!-- §29 -->
<div class="sn">§ 29 — <i>Chanukah</i> = Training/Initiation</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Chanukah</span> is called <span class="h">Chanukah</span> [initiation/training] because this is the essential training for entering the service of Hashem: drawing upon oneself the illumination from the root of one's <span class="h">neshamah</span> in Torah, which flows through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> — which is the essence of kindling <span class="h">ner Chanukah</span>.</div>

<!-- §30 -->
<div class="sn">§ 30 — Traveling to the Graves of True <i>Tzadikim</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">tzadik</span> who engaged all his life in <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span> — teaching Torah for the sake of teaching, arousing root-souls, returning many from sin, magnifying Hashem's <span class="h">kavod</span> — even after his passing, continues this work. For every <span class="h">tzadik</span>, according to his share in Torah, when he departs — his share in Torah departs with him. (Therefore, when a <span class="h">tzadik</span> passes, it is difficult to teach Torah — as I heard from Rabbainu's holy mouth when a certain <span class="h">tzadik</span> departed.)</div>

<div class="p">The great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in whom all Torah is encompassed — when they pass, the root of all Torah = the root of all Israel's souls departs with them. But because of their love for Israel, having devoted their entire lives to illuminating souls, they do not allow the light to depart entirely. <span class="kc">Even after their passing, they continue to draw and illuminate for every single Jew the illumination from the root of his <span class="h">neshamah</span> in Torah.</span> The greatness of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>: even when he ascends to the highest heights, he is also below, drawing illumination to all below (as in <span class="lr">LM II:7</span>). The illumination of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s <span class="h">nefesh</span> remains upon his holy grave, shining throughout the World of <span class="h">Asiyah</span> (Arizal).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, when one comes to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s grave — where his <span class="h">nefesh</span> rests with all the Torah he engaged in his entire life, through which he aroused root-souls — <span class="kc">one draws upon oneself the illumination from the root of one's <span class="h">neshamah</span> in Torah</span>. For there all Israel's souls are encompassed, rooted in Torah. Through this, people return to good.</div>

<!-- §31 -->
<div class="sn">§ 31 — Chizkiyahu: The <i>Yeshivah</i> on the Grave, the <i>Sefer Torah</i> on the Grave</div>

<div class="p">The Sages (<span class="h">Bava Kamma</span> 17a) about Chizkiyahu King of Yehudah: <span class="mx">"they gave him honor in his death"</span> — they established a <span class="h">yeshivah</span> on his grave and placed a <span class="h">Sefer Torah</span> there, saying: <span class="mx">"This one fulfilled what is written herein."</span> They concluded that for others we say "fulfilled" — but for Chizkiyahu we also say "taught," because he engaged in <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span>, teaching Torah to others by arousing their root-souls.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore he merited this honor: a <span class="h">yeshivah</span> on his grave — demonstrating that all Torah-institutions are built and founded upon the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s grave, from which the illumination of root-souls shines. The <span class="h">Sefer Torah</span> on his grave shows that the Torah's root is encompassed in the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s soul. All this is derived from <span class="v">"they gave him honor in his death"</span> — for the essential <span class="h">kavod</span> is when Torah is taught to others, returning them in <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, raising Hashem's <span class="h">kavod</span>: <span class="v">"those who honor Me, I will honor."</span></div>

<!-- §32 -->
<div class="sn">§ 32 — "<i>Tzadikim</i> Are Greater in Death Than in Life"</div>

<div class="p">The Sages: <span class="mx">"<span class="h">tzadikim</span> are greater in their death than in their life."</span> On the verse: <span class="v">"I praise the dead who have already died"</span> — applied to Dovid HaMelech. When Shlomo wished to bring the <span class="h">Aron</span> into the <span class="h">Kodesh HaKodashim</span>, the gates clung together and would not open. He said twenty-four <span class="h">r'nanos</span> [supplications] and was not answered — until he said: <span class="v">"remember the kindnesses of Dovid Your servant."</span></div>

<div class="p">Dovid labored all his life in <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span> — <span class="h">chasdei Dovid hane'emanim</span> [the faithful kindnesses of Dovid]. He was <span class="h">masik shma'atsa aliba d'hilchasa</span> — he brought Torah-discussions to their definitive <span class="h">halachah</span>: <span class="v">"Hashem was with him"</span> = the <span class="h">halachah</span> followed him. The true perfection of <span class="h">halachah</span> = one's Torah-study arouses root-souls so that illumination reaches each person, showing him the way to leave his evil and draw close to Hashem. <span class="h">Halachos</span> are called "walkings" [<span class="h">halichos</span>]: <span class="mx">"do not read <span class="h">halichos</span> but <span class="h">halachos</span>"</span> — through <span class="h">halachos</span>, one merits to walk in Hashem's ways. Dovid cried: <span class="v">"how can a youth purify his path? By keeping Your word!"</span></div>

<div class="p">Through this, Dovid merited to find the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> = the place of the <span class="h">Aron</span>: <span class="v">"I will not give sleep to my eyes… until I find a place for Hashem."</span> When the <span class="h">Aron</span> stands in its fixed place, the Torah's illumination shines from its root in full power, reaching each individual according to his soul-root. Though the <span class="h">Baal Davar</span> later incited Israel until the Temple was destroyed — twice — <span class="kc">the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> that Dovid found can never be undone</span>: <span class="v">"why do you mountains leap? The mountain Hashem desired for His dwelling — indeed, Hashem will dwell there forever."</span> Since the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> rested there, it never rested elsewhere. The <span class="h">tikkun</span>'s imprint remains forever. Through it, the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of every generation can draw the illumination even now.</div>

<!-- §33 -->
<div class="sn">§ 33 — Why the Gates Clung Shut — and How They Opened</div>

<div class="p">Why did the gates refuse to let the <span class="h">Aron</span> enter? The gates saw that the Temple would eventually be destroyed — twice. They thought: if it will be destroyed and the <span class="h">Aron</span> will have no home, why should it enter? All of Shlomo's prayers and supplications failed — until he said: <span class="v">"remember the kindnesses of Dovid Your servant."</span></div>

<div class="p">He invoked the power and merit of Dovid who was <span class="kc">already dead</span>. Despite this, his <span class="h">tikkun</span> — finding the place of the <span class="h">Aron</span> — can never be undone. The building can be destroyed, but the <span class="kc">place</span> that Dovid found remains fixed forever. Since this <span class="h">tikkun</span> is indestructible, eventually Dovid/<span class="h">Mashiach</span> will complete what he began — returning all Israel to good through the Torah's illumination shining from the <span class="h">Aron</span>'s place. When the gates understood this, they immediately opened and admitted the <span class="h">Aron</span> to stand on its place prepared for eternity.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Remember the kindnesses [<span class="h">chasdei</span>] of Dovid"</span> — <span class="mx"><span class="h">chasdei Dovid</span></span> specifically = the <span class="h">halachos</span> = <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span>: what Dovid learned in order to teach, arousing root-souls — through which he merited to find the <span class="h">Aron</span>'s place.</div>

<!-- §34 -->
<div class="sn">§ 34 — "Hashem Loves the Gates of Tziyon": <i>Shearim Ha'Metzuyanim Ba'Halachah</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Hashem loves the gates of Tziyon more than all the dwellings of Yaakov."</span> The Sages (<span class="h">Berachos</span> 8a): <span class="mx">"Hashem loves gates distinguished by <span class="h">halachah</span> more than all the <span class="h">batei knesses</span> and <span class="h">batei midrash</span>."</span></div>

<div class="p">But don't all <span class="h">batei midrash</span> study <span class="h">halachos</span>? The point: the true perfection is Torah-study that arouses root-souls, returning people to good. Such <span class="h">tzadikim</span> create new <span class="kc">gates</span> and open all existing gates for drawing near to Hashem. The <span class="h">halachos</span> create <span class="h">tziyunim</span> [markers/signs] pointing to these gates — for the gates were made through them. Therefore: <span class="mx">"gates distinguished by <span class="h">halachah</span>"</span> = gates whose <span class="h">halachos</span> serve as signposts. Hashem loves them more than all other <span class="h">batei midrash</span> — because <span class="mx">"not study but action is the main thing."</span> Hashem loves the Torah-study that leads to action: creating gates to draw near to Hashem. These are the gates of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, through which people came to know Him. The <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> = <span class="h">da'as</span>; the gates and openings in one's <span class="h">da'as</span> through which the knowledge of Hashem enters: <span class="v">"lift up your heads, O gates… and the King of Glory shall enter!"</span></div>

<!-- §35 -->
<div class="sn">§ 35 — "Four <i>Amos</i> of <i>Halachah</i>"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Berachos</span> 8a: <span class="mx">"since the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, Hashem has nothing in His world except four <span class="h">amos</span> of <span class="h">halachah</span> alone."</span> Four <span class="h">amos</span> = a person's space, derived from the four letters of the <span class="h">Shem Havayah</span>. Every person, wherever he is, has four <span class="h">amos</span> in which to draw near to Hashem. These are merited through <span class="h">halachos</span> — through the power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who arouse root-souls through Torah. In the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, this was fully accomplished; now, whoever merits four <span class="h">amos</span> of <span class="h">halachah</span> — finding his place of connection to Hashem wherever he is — this is infinitely precious to Hashem. It is His ultimate <span class="h">kavod</span>: that even the most distant ones draw near.</div>

<!-- §36 -->
<div class="sn">§ 36 — Moshe and the Bones of Yosef: Torah and the <i>Tzadik</i>'s Grave</div>

<div class="p">Moshe exerted himself to take Yosef's bones: <span class="v">"Moshe took the bones of Yosef with him."</span> The Sages: <span class="mx">"the wise of heart takes <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — this is Moshe."</span> Moshe = the totality of Torah. He needed to draw the Torah's illumination and give it to Israel. Therefore he exerted himself for Yosef's coffin — the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s grave, from which Torah's illumination shines from its root. The two <span class="h">aronos</span> [arks] — Yosef's coffin and the <span class="h">Aron HaEdus</span> — traveled side by side, for each depends on the other: the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s grave and the Torah's <span class="h">Aron</span>.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- §37 -->
<div class="sn">§ 37 — Returning to <i>HaMotzi</i>: Baking = <i>Birur</i> Through Torah</div>

<div class="p">We return to the subject we began with. The Sages' answer — <span class="mx">"from where the baking began [<span class="h">mei'haicha d'kadim bishula</span>]"</span> — is explained: baking and cooking on fire = a form of <span class="h">birur</span> [clarification]. Because of Adam's eating-sin, all food — especially bread — fell into the <span class="h">kelippah</span> and needs many labors before it is fit for human consumption. Baking in fire destroys and burns the evil clinging to the food before the oven: <span class="mx">"from fire they came forth, and fire shall consume them."</span> The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> = the Torah, called fire: <span class="v">"are not My words like fire, declares Hashem, and like a hammer shattering rock!"</span></div>

<div class="p">All the <span class="h">birur</span>-stages of grain/bread are accomplished through Torah = the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> embedded in each stage. There are ten <span class="h">mitzvos</span> in bread: do not plow with ox and donkey together, do not sow <span class="h">kil'ayim</span>, etc. <span class="v">"Your Torah is within my innards"</span> — Rashi: <span class="mx">"all my food is according to Your word."</span> The food's <span class="h">tikkun</span> is through Torah.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one breaks <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> from where baking began: baking = the completion of external <span class="h">birur</span> (the bread becoming fit for human consumption). The part that baked first = where the external <span class="h">birur</span> was completed first. From there one must begin the <span class="kc">internal <span class="h">birur</span></span> — the eating-clarification inside the body, which is the ultimate <span class="h">birur</span>. The internal must begin from where the external concluded.</div>

<!-- §38 -->
<div class="sn">§ 38 — The Answer to All Obstacles: Torah from Beginning to End</div>

<div class="p">The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> is through Torah from beginning to end. The question <span class="mx">"from where do you break <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>?"</span> really asks: how does one accomplish the ultimate <span class="h">birur</span> — purifying good from literal filth — when the obstacles intensify terrifyingly, especially near the end of one's spiritual completion? (As Rabbainu said: <span class="mx">"there are those who are already at the door and turn back from there"</span> — <span class="lr">LM II:48</span>.)</div>

<div class="p">The answer: <span class="mx"><span class="h">mei'haicha d'kadim bishula</span></span> — from where the clarification began. Just as the word "baking" [<span class="h">bishul</span>] also refers to all earlier stages of growth (the Sages commonly call the ripening of fruits <span class="h">bishul</span>: <span class="mx">"from when they begin to ripen [<span class="h">l'hisbashal</span>] on the tree"</span>), so too: the <span class="kc">final <span class="h">tikkun</span> comes from the same place as the first <span class="h">tikkun</span></span> = Torah. Torah preceded the world (<span class="h">kadmah la'olam</span>); from Torah comes the beginning of all <span class="h">tikkunim</span>, and from Torah comes their completion. One must be immensely strong, never allowing himself to be broken from Torah no matter what he endures. <span class="v">"The fear of Hashem is pure, standing forever"</span> — the Torah, called "the pure fear of Hashem," stands forever. At every moment, through Torah, one can overcome. As the clarification began, so it ends: <span class="kc">from Torah — through Torah — to Torah</span>.</div>

<!-- §39 -->
<div class="sn">§ 39 — Yosef's Blessing: "From the Peak of the Ancient Mountains"</div>

<div class="p">Yosef was blessed: <span class="v">"of the blessing of Hashem is his land — from the bounty of heaven, from the dew, from the deep crouching below, from the bounty of… and from the peak of the ancient mountains [<span class="h">mei'rosh harrei kedem</span>]."</span> All these "bounties" (<span class="h">megadim</span>) = all the clarifications through which the good and sweetness of food is extracted = the essential blessing reversing Adam's curse. Rashi: <span class="mx">"his mountains ripen their fruits first"</span> — all the <span class="h">birur</span>-stages of food are completed from the place where clarification began. <span class="v">"From the peak of the ancient mountains"</span> = from the place where ripening/baking first began = through the power of Torah, which is called <span class="h">kedem</span> [ancient/primordial]: <span class="v">"from the beginning, from before the earth"</span> (Mishlei 8).</div>

<div class="p">Yosef, who guarded the Torah, merited that his mountains ripen first — and from there all the <span class="h">megadim</span> (= <span class="h">birur</span>-stages) flow, right through to the ultimate <span class="h">birur</span> of eating and satiation. For just as the beginning of <span class="h">tikkun</span> is through Torah, so is the end. One must hold fast to Torah and service always — and then one will certainly merit a good end: the completion of all <span class="h">tikkun</span> and <span class="h">birur</span>.</div>

<!-- §40 -->
<div class="sn">§ 40 — Yosef's <i>Tikkun</i>: Stripping Soiled Garments Through the Test</div>

<div class="p">Yosef specifically was blessed with this because the essential receiving of Torah depends on Yosef, as explained: <span class="v">"Moshe took the bones of Yosef with him."</span> Yosef merited to strip the soiled garments completely by <span class="kc">standing firm in the test</span> — the seduction by a married woman, maidservant, gentile, harlot — the very source of soiled garments.</div>

<div class="p">Yaakov, seeing Yosef's righteousness, made him a <span class="h">k'sones passim</span> [fine garment] = clean garments, white garments. <span class="v">"Because he was a <span class="h">ben zekunim</span> [son of old age] — a <span class="h">bar chakim</span> [wise son]"</span>: Yaakov transmitted all his wisdom and Torah to him. Therefore: <span class="v">"he made him a <span class="h">k'sones passim</span>"</span> = <span class="h">chaluka d'rabbanan</span> [the rabbinical robe] = clean garments merited through Torah. But his brothers were jealous, and the Sages criticized Yaakov — it was premature: Yosef still needed to endure his tests. The brothers, being prophetically aware, saw the <span class="h">Baal Davar</span> still contending with him and mistakenly thought he was not clean. Therefore: <span class="v">"they stripped Yosef of his tunic… and dipped it in blood"</span> — showing his garments were still soiled.</div>

<div class="p">When Yaakov saw the bloodied garment: <span class="v">"my son's tunic! A wild beast devoured him!"</span> — the Sages: this alludes to Potiphar's wife. Yaakov immediately understood: the soiled garments were still contending with Yosef, and their primary assault is through sexual desire. In the moment of the test: <span class="v">"she grabbed him by his garment"</span> — <span class="mx">"by his garment"</span> specifically! She sought to seize the soiled garments clinging to him. But Yosef stood firm: <span class="v">"he left his garment in her hand and fled outside."</span> <span class="kc">At that very moment he stripped from himself completely all grip of the soiled garments</span> — leaving them in her hand. Even great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, until they stand in their destined test, retain some trace of the soiled garments.</div>

<div class="p">Afterward he merited clean and precious garments: <span class="v">"he clothed him in garments of fine linen."</span> The Zohar: <span class="mx">"Yosef rose from below…"</span> From the greatness of his righteousness he merited to draw clean garments upon all his brothers: <span class="v">"to each he gave changes of clothing"</span> — hinting that he had the power to rectify them all. <span class="v">"To Binyamin he gave… five changes of clothing"</span> — alluding to the five garment-changes of the <span class="h">Kohein Gadol</span> on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, who entered the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> built in Binyamin's portion.</div>

<div class="p">Yosef's greatness came through interpreting dreams — because he merited to clarify his soul from the aspect of <span class="v">"I am asleep"</span> (= <span class="h">palga</span>). He rose to power through interpreting Pharaoh's dream of the seven years of plenty and seven of famine: the essential <span class="h">birur</span> = the <span class="h">birur</span> of eating and satiation = clarifying plenty from famine. His advice — <span class="v">"let them gather grain"</span> — the word <span class="h">bar</span> [grain] hints at 202 of the 288 sparks. Yosef gathered sparks in Mitzrayim. His advice to every person seeking to preserve his soul: seize Torah, prayer, and good deeds in the years of plenty (when awakening is felt), storing them for the years of famine (when darkness returns). <span class="v">"Whatever your hand finds to do with your strength — do it!"</span></div>

<div class="p">Israel's survival throughout the exile of Mitzrayim — and all exiles, especially this final bitter one — depends on Yosef/<span class="h">tzadikim</span> who stood in countless tests and left behind wondrous teachings. Through them, illumination from our root-souls is drawn in countless wondrous ways: <span class="v">"to preserve for you a remnant on earth, and to sustain you as a great deliverance."</span></div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 3 (§§ 27–40) ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§27-29</strong>: The Name <em>Nachal</em> (נ.ח.ל) = acronym of lighting <em>ner Chanukah</em> = the Torah as a flowing stream that never ceases. The <em>Menorah</em> = stubbornness in Hashem's service. <em>Chanukah</em> = initiation into service through the <em>tzadik</em>'s illumination.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§30-31 — Graves of <em>tzadikim</em></strong>: Even after passing, the <em>tzadik</em> continues illuminating root-souls from his grave. Chizkiyahu: a <em>yeshivah</em> and <em>Sefer Torah</em> on his grave — because he taught <em>Toras Chesed</em>. All Torah institutions are built on the <em>tzadik</em>'s grave.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§32-33 — Dovid and the <em>Aron</em></strong>: Dovid labored to find the Temple's place. The gates clung shut because the Temple would be destroyed — but Dovid's <em>tikkun</em> (the <em>place</em>) can never be undone. "Remember <em>chasdei</em> Dovid" = <em>Toras Chesed</em> = <em>halachos</em>. The gates opened.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§34-36</strong>: "Gates distinguished by <em>halachah</em>" = gates that show the way to Hashem. "Four <em>amos</em> of <em>halachah</em>" = everyone has a place to connect, wherever he is. Moshe took Yosef's bones = Torah and the <em>tzadik</em>'s grave travel together.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§37-38 — "From where the baking began"</strong>: Baking = the completion of external <em>birur</em> through Torah-fire. Break <em>HaMotzi</em> from there = the internal <em>birur</em> begins where the external completed. The answer to every obstacle: <strong>Torah from beginning to end.</strong></p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§39-40 — Yosef</strong>: Blessed that "his mountains ripen first" = Torah (<em>kedem</em>) is the source of all <em>birur</em>. Yosef stripped the soiled garments through the test. "She grabbed him by his garment" — he left the soiled garments in her hand. Afterward: fine linen = clean garments. His dream-interpretation = clarifying "I am asleep." <em>Bar</em> = 202 of 288 sparks. Advice: seize good deeds in years of plenty for years of famine.</p>
</div>

